	"FriendlyName": "HeatSink - Weapon Heat, Overheat, Venting & Jams",
	"Description": "A heat-based weapon is a rhythm, and the rhythm is set by four rules that are easy to get subtly wrong. Overheating is a LOCKOUT with its own duration, not 'wait until heat drops below the maximum' - otherwise the weapon comes back the instant heat falls one point and the player learns to tap at the boundary. Cooling starts after a delay that every shot resets, or heat quietly vanishes between the rounds of a burst. The shot that crosses the maximum still fires and then overheats, because refusing a trigger pull at ninety-nine percent heat reads as a broken gun. And a manual vent is a real trade: it is faster than waiting, but it costs a window in which you cannot shoot. Jams are a separate state with their own clearing time, with a chance that climbs only above a threshold you choose. Cooling is linear, so two half steps and one whole step agree and a frame-rate change does not change the weapon. Every rule is a pure function the component and the tests both call.",
